select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 10, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.0", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.0 Overview", "body": "Breeden, D. T., and Litzenberger, R. H. (1978). Prices of state-contingent claims implicit in option prices. Journal of Business, 51(4), 621–651.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 12.\nMcDonald, R. L. (2013). Derivatives Markets (3rd ed.). Pearson. Chapter 3.\nMcMillan, L. G. (2012). Options as a Strategic Investment (5th ed.). Prentice Hall.\nNatenberg, S. (2015). Option Volatility and Pricing (2nd ed.). McGraw-Hill."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.0", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nHow likely is a rise, and what is the call worth?\nNorthwind shares trade at $28. In three months the price will be either $32.20 or $25.20; no other outcome is possible. A European call with a strike of $28 expires on that date and will therefore pay either $4.20 or nothing. The risk-free rate is 5 percent per year with continuous compounding. Select the probability that the reader believes should be assigned to a rise, and observe the value that this belief implies for the call.\nThe value of the call does not depend on the probability of a rise. Two investors who disagree entirely about the prospects of Northwind must agree that the call is worth $1.87, because either of them can manufacture it for that amount. This result, which is the central idea of the theory of option valuation, is derived in Section 10.1. The probability at which the two lines cross, 45 percent, is not a forecast; Section 10.2 explains what it is."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.1", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.1", "heading": "A one-step binomial model", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.1 A one-step binomial model", "body": "A binomial tree is a diagram of the paths that the price of a share may follow, on the assumption that in each short interval of time the price either rises by a given proportion or falls by a given proportion. The assumption is restrictive for a single interval, but when the life of an option is divided into many short intervals the tree approximates realistic behavior closely, as Section 10.6 shows. This section treats the opening exercise in detail."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.1", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.1", "heading": "Valuation by replication", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.1 Valuation by replication", "body": "The call pays $4.20 if the share price rises to $32.20 and nothing if it falls to $25.20. Consider a portfolio consisting of \\(\\Delta\\) shares, financed in part by borrowing an amount \\(B\\) at the risk-free rate. The aim is to choose \\(\\Delta\\) and \\(B\\) so that the portfolio is worth exactly the same as the call in both outcomes. After three months the loan requires repayment of \\(Be^{0.05\\times0.25}=1.012578B\\), so the two conditions are\n\\[ 32.20\\,\\Delta-1.012578\\,B=4.20, \\qquad 25.20\\,\\Delta-1.012578\\,B=0 . \\]\nSubtracting the second from the first gives \\(7.00\\,\\Delta=4.20\\), so \\(\\Delta=0.60\\). The second equation then gives \\(B=25.20\\times0.60/1.012578=\\$14.93\\). A portfolio of 0.60 shares with borrowing of $14.93 is worth $4.20 if the share rises and nothing if it falls: it replicates the call. Its cost today is\n\\[ 0.60\\times28.00-14.93=16.80-14.93=\\$1.87 . \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.1", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.1", "heading": "Valuation by replication", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.1 Valuation by replication", "body": "By Principle 1.1, the call must be worth $1.87. If it were quoted at $2.20, an arbitrageur would write the call, buy 0.60 shares, and borrow $14.93, receiving \\(2.20-1.87=\\$0.33\\) today with no obligation in either outcome. If it were quoted at $1.50, the arbitrageur would buy the call, sell 0.60 shares short, and invest $14.93, receiving $0.37.\nThe number of shares, \\(\\Delta\\), is the delta of the option: the change in the value of the option divided by the change in the price of the share between the two outcomes, \\((4.20-0)/(32.20-25.20)=0.60\\). The argument may be stated in the opposite direction. A portfolio that is long 0.60 shares and short one call is worth \\(25.20\\times0.60=\\$15.12\\) in both outcomes; it is riskless, and must therefore earn the risk-free rate. Its value today is \\(15.12e^{-0.0125}=\\$14.93\\), so that \\(16.80-c=14.93\\) and \\(c=\\$1.87\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.1#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.1", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.1", "heading": "Generalization", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.1 Generalization", "body": "Let the share price be \\(S_0\\) and the life of the option \\(T\\). The price moves either up to \\(S_0u\\) or down to \\(S_0d\\), where \\(u>1\\) and \\(d<1\\), and the option then pays \\(f_u\\) or \\(f_d\\). The replicating portfolio holds \\(\\Delta\\) shares, where\n\\[ \\Delta=\\frac{f_u-f_d}{S_0u-S_0d}. \\]\nSolving for the borrowing and computing the cost of the portfolio gives the value of the option:\nResult 10.1 — One-step binomial valuation\n\\[ f=e^{-rT}\\left[\\,p\\,f_u+(1-p)\\,f_d\\,\\right], \\qquad\\text{where}\\qquad p=\\frac{e^{rT}-d}{u-d}. \\]\nIn the example, \\(u=32.20/28=1.15\\), \\(d=25.20/28=0.90\\), and \\(p=(1.012578-0.90)/(1.15-0.90)=0.4503\\). Then \\(f=0.987578\\times(0.4503\\times4.20+0.5497\\times0)=\\$1.87\\), as before. The formula applies to any derivative whose payoff depends only on the share price at \\(T\\). For a put with a strike of $28, \\(f_u=0\\) and \\(f_d=2.80\\), and \\(f=0.987578\\times0.5497\\times2.80=\\$1.52\\), which satisfies put–call parity: \\(1.87+28e^{-0.0125}-28=\\$1.52\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.1#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.1", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.1", "heading": "Generalization", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.1 Generalization", "body": "Absence of arbitrage requires \\(d<e^{rT}<u\\). If the share were certain to earn more than the risk-free rate, or certain to earn less, one of the two assets would dominate the other. The condition ensures that \\(p\\) lies between 0 and 1."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.1#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.1", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.1", "heading": "Generalization", "kind": "warning", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.1 Generalization", "body": "Common error\nThe formula of Result 10.1 does not contain the probability that the share price rises. This is not an omission. The probability affects the price of the share, \\(S_0\\), which is taken as given; once \\(S_0\\) is known, nothing further about the probability is needed to value the option relative to the share. An investor who believes a rise to be very likely should regard both the share and the call as attractive at their present prices, and cannot conclude that the call is underpriced relative to the share."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.2", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.2", "heading": "Risk-neutral valuation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.2 Risk-neutral valuation", "body": "The quantity \\(p\\) in Result 10.1 has the form of a probability, and the expression \\(pf_u+(1-p)f_d\\) has the form of an expected payoff. The interpretation is exact in a particular sense. Suppose that the probability of a rise were \\(p\\). The expected share price at \\(T\\) would be\n\\[ p\\,S_0u+(1-p)\\,S_0d=S_0\\left[p(u-d)+d\\right]=S_0\\,e^{rT}. \\]\nWith probability \\(p\\), the share is expected to earn exactly the risk-free rate. A world in which every asset is expected to earn the risk-free rate is one in which investors require no compensation for bearing risk; it is called a risk-neutral world, and \\(p\\) is the risk-neutral probability of a rise.\nPrinciple 10.1 — Risk-neutral valuation\nThe value of a derivative is its expected payoff in a risk-neutral world, discounted at the risk-free rate. To apply the principle: assume that the expected return on the underlying asset is the risk-free rate; compute the expected payoff of the derivative under that assumption; and discount it at the risk-free rate. The value obtained is correct in the real world, and not only in a risk-neutral one."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.2#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.2", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.2", "heading": "Risk-neutral valuation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.2 Risk-neutral valuation", "body": "The principle does not assert that investors are indifferent to risk or that shares are in fact expected to earn the risk-free rate. It is a method of calculation whose validity rests on the replication argument of Section 10.1: because the option can be replicated, its value relative to the share is the same whatever investors' attitudes to risk, and it may therefore be computed under whichever attitude makes the computation simplest."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.2#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.2", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.2", "heading": "The real world", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.2 The real world", "body": "Suppose that investors in fact expect Northwind shares to return 12 percent per year. The real-world probability of a rise, \\(q\\), satisfies \\(28e^{0.12\\times0.25}=32.20q+25.20(1-q)\\), which gives \\(q=0.5218\\). The expected payoff of the call in the real world is \\(0.5218\\times4.20=\\$2.19\\). To obtain the correct value of $1.87 from this figure, it would have to be discounted at the rate that solves \\(1.87=2.19e^{-0.25k}\\), namely \\(k=64\\) percent per year. The call is far riskier than the share, since it is equivalent to 0.60 shares financed largely by borrowing, and investors require a correspondingly higher return on it. That return is difficult to determine directly and, as later sections show, it changes from node to node. Risk-neutral valuation avoids the problem altogether.\nConcept check 10.1\nA share trades at $50. In six months it will be worth $55 or $45. The risk-free rate is 4 percent with continuous compounding. What is the value of a six-month European call with a strike of $50?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.3", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.3", "heading": "Two-step trees", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.3 Two-step trees", "body": "The analysis extends to a tree with two steps by repeated use of Result 10.1, working backward from the expiration date. Let each step last \\(\\Delta t\\) years, so that \\(p=(e^{r\\Delta t}-d)/(u-d)\\) and the discount factor for one step is \\(e^{-r\\Delta t}\\). The value at each node is computed from the values at the two nodes that follow it. This procedure is backward induction."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.3", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.3", "heading": "Two-step trees", "kind": "example", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.3 Two-step trees", "body": "Worked example 10.1 — A European call on a two-step tree\nNorthwind shares trade at $28. In each of two three-month steps the price rises by 15 percent or falls by 10 percent. The risk-free rate is 5 percent. Value a six-month European call with a strike of $28.\n1\nShare prices. After one step: $32.20 or $25.20. After two: \\(28\\times1.15^{2}=\\$37.03\\); \\(28\\times1.15\\times0.90=\\$28.98\\); \\(28\\times0.90^{2}=\\$22.68\\).\n2\nPayoffs at expiration. \\(f_{uu}=37.03-28=9.03\\); \\(f_{ud}=28.98-28=0.98\\); \\(f_{dd}=0\\).\n3\nParameters. \\(p=0.4503\\); \\(1-p=0.5497\\); \\(e^{-r\\Delta t}=0.987578\\).\n4\nUpper node ($32.20). \\(f_u=0.987578\\times(0.4503\\times9.03+0.5497\\times0.98)=\\$4.548\\).\n5\nLower node ($25.20). \\(f_d=0.987578\\times(0.4503\\times0.98+0.5497\\times0)=\\$0.436\\).\n6\nInitial node. \\(f=0.987578\\times(0.4503\\times4.548+0.5497\\times0.436)=\\$2.26\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.3", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.3", "heading": "Two-step trees", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.3 Two-step trees", "body": "For a European option the intermediate nodes may be bypassed. The risk-neutral probabilities of reaching the three final nodes are \\(p^{2}\\), \\(2p(1-p)\\), and \\((1-p)^{2}\\), and\n\\[ f=e^{-2r\\Delta t}\\left[p^{2}f_{uu}+2p(1-p)f_{ud}+(1-p)^{2}f_{dd}\\right], \\]\nwhich for the example gives \\(0.975310\\times(0.2028\\times9.03+0.4951\\times0.98)=\\$2.26\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.3#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.3", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.3", "heading": "Delta on the tree", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.3 Delta on the tree", "body": "The delta of the call differs from node to node. At the initial node, \\(\\Delta=(4.548-0.436)/(32.20-25.20)=0.587\\). At the upper node after one step, \\(\\Delta=(9.03-0.98)/(37.03-28.98)=1.000\\); at the lower node, \\(\\Delta=(0.98-0)/(28.98-22.68)=0.156\\). A portfolio that replicates the call must therefore be adjusted after the first step: if the share price has risen, the holding is increased from 0.587 to 1.000 shares; if it has fallen, the holding is reduced to 0.156 shares. The adjustment requires no new money, because the value of the portfolio at each node equals the cost of the portfolio required for the next step. A strategy of this kind is self-financing, and the practice of adjusting a hedge as the share price changes is dynamic hedging, the subject of Chapter 14."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.4#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.4", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.4", "heading": "American options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.4 American options", "body": "The holder of an American option may exercise at any node. The value of the option at a node is therefore the greater of two amounts: the value obtained by exercising immediately, and the value of keeping the option for a further step, which is given by Result 10.1 and is called the continuation value. The procedure is again backward induction, with one additional comparison at each node:\n\\[ f=\\max\\!\\left(\\text{payoff from immediate exercise},\\ \\ e^{-r\\Delta t}\\left[p\\,f_u+(1-p)\\,f_d\\right]\\right). \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.4", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.4", "heading": "American options", "kind": "example", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.4 American options", "body": "Worked example 10.2 — An American put on a two-step tree\nOn the tree of Worked example 10.1, value a six-month put with a strike of $30, (a) if it is European and (b) if it is American.\n1\nPayoffs at expiration. \\(f_{uu}=0\\); \\(f_{ud}=30-28.98=1.02\\); \\(f_{dd}=30-22.68=7.32\\).\n2\nUpper node ($32.20). Continuation value: \\(0.987578\\times(0.4503\\times0+0.5497\\times1.02)=\\$0.554\\). Immediate exercise would yield \\(30-32.20<0\\). The put is not exercised: value $0.554 for both styles.\n3\nLower node ($25.20). Continuation value: \\(0.987578\\times(0.4503\\times1.02+0.5497\\times7.32)=\\$4.427\\). Immediate exercise yields \\(30-25.20=\\$4.80\\), which is greater. The European put is worth $4.427 at this node; the American put is exercised and is worth $4.80.\n4\n(a) European put. \\(0.987578\\times(0.4503\\times0.554+0.5497\\times4.427)=\\$2.65\\).\n5\n(b) American put. Continuation value at the initial node: \\(0.987578\\times(0.4503\\times0.554+0.5497\\times4.80)=\\$2.85\\). Immediate exercise yields \\(30-28=\\$2.00\\), which is less; the put is held. Value: $2.85.\n6"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.4#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.4", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.4", "heading": "American options", "kind": "example", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.4 American options", "body": "Conclusion. The right to exercise early is worth \\(2.85-2.65=\\$0.20\\). It arises entirely at the lower node, where the holder gains more from receiving $30 at once than from waiting."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.4#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.4", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.4", "heading": "American options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.4 American options", "body": "If the same procedure is applied to an American call on a share that pays no dividend, the continuation value exceeds the exercise value at every node, in accordance with Result 8.3, and the American and European values coincide.\nConcept check 10.2\nAt a node of a binomial tree the share price is $36 and an American put has a strike of $42. The values of the put at the two following nodes are $2.40 (up) and $9.60 (down); \\(p=0.6523\\) and the one-step discount factor is 0.9704. What is the value of the put at this node?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.5#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.5", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.5", "heading": "Matching the tree to volatility", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.5 Matching the tree to volatility", "body": "In the examples so far, the proportions \\(u\\) and \\(d\\) were given. In practice they are chosen to match the volatility of the share, \\(\\sigma\\), introduced in Section 8.2. Volatility is defined so that the standard deviation of the return on the share over a short interval of length \\(\\Delta t\\) is \\(\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\); a volatility of 39 percent per year implies a standard deviation of return of \\(0.39\\times\\sqrt{0.25}=19.5\\) percent over three months.\nResult 10.2 — The Cox–Ross–Rubinstein tree\nA binomial tree with steps of length \\(\\Delta t\\) matches a volatility of \\(\\sigma\\) when \\[ u=e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}},\\qquad d=\\frac1u=e^{-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}},\\qquad p=\\frac{a-d}{u-d},\\qquad a=e^{r\\Delta t}. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.5#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.5", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.5", "heading": "Matching the tree to volatility", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.5 Matching the tree to volatility", "body": "Because \\(d=1/u\\), an upward movement followed by a downward movement returns the share price to its starting value, and the tree recombines: after \\(n\\) steps there are \\(n+1\\) distinct share prices and not \\(2^{n}\\). The choice of \\(u\\) and \\(d\\) does not involve the expected return on the share, in accordance with Section 10.2; the volatility of the share is the same in the real world and in the risk-neutral world, and only the probability of a rise differs between them.\nWith \\(\\sigma=0.39\\) and \\(\\Delta t=0.25\\): \\(u=e^{0.195}=1.2153\\), \\(d=0.8228\\), \\(a=1.012578\\), and \\(p=(1.012578-0.8228)/(1.2153-0.8228)=0.4835\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.5#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.5", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.5", "heading": "Matching the tree to volatility", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.5 Matching the tree to volatility", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 10.1 — A binomial tree\nShare prices, option values, and early exercise at every node\nThe tree values a six-month option on Northwind shares (\\(S_0=\\$28\\), \\(r=5\\) percent) with the parameters of Result 10.2. Select the type and style of option, the strike, the volatility, and the number of steps. Nodes at which an American option should be exercised are shaded. Work one node by hand with Result 10.1 and compare."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.6#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.6", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.6", "heading": "Increasing the number of steps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.6 Increasing the number of steps", "body": "A tree with one or two steps is a device for exposition. In practice the life of an option is divided into thirty or more steps, and often several hundred. With thirty steps there are 31 possible share prices at expiration and more than a thousand million paths by which they may be reached. As the number of steps increases, the distribution of the share price at expiration on the tree approaches a continuous distribution, and the value of a European option approaches a limit. That limit is the Black–Scholes–Merton formula, derived in Chapter 12.\nInteractive figure 10.2 shows the approach. The binomial value does not converge smoothly: it oscillates as the number of steps alternates between odd and even, because the position of the strike price relative to the final nodes changes. A common remedy is to average the values obtained with \\(n\\) and \\(n+1\\) steps."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.6#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.6", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.6", "heading": "Increasing the number of steps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.6 Increasing the number of steps", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 10.2 — Convergence\nThe binomial value approaches a limit as the number of steps increases\nThe figure uses the option selected in Interactive figure 10.1. For a European option the dashed line is the Black–Scholes–Merton value; for an American option, for which no formula exists, it is the value from a tree with 400 steps."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.6#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.6", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.6", "heading": "The early-exercise region", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.6 The early-exercise region", "body": "With many steps, the tree identifies for each date the share price below which an American put should be exercised. Interactive figure 10.3 presents the result as a surface. Where the American surface lies above the plane of intrinsic value, the put should be held; where it coincides with that plane, the put should be exercised. The European surface, shown for comparison, dips below intrinsic value at low share prices, as Section 8.5 predicted."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.6#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.6", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.6", "heading": "The early-exercise region", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.6 The early-exercise region", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 10.3 — Three-dimensional view\nThe value of a put, American and European, and the region of early exercise\nThe put has a strike of $30; the risk-free rate is 5 percent and the volatility 39 percent. The dark line at the front edge is the payoff at expiration. Select the style, then lower the share price with a year remaining until the readout reports that immediate exercise is optimal. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.7#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.7", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.7", "heading": "Options on other assets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.7 Options on other assets", "body": "The tree is easily adapted to underlying assets that provide income. In a risk-neutral world the total return on any asset is the risk-free rate \\(r\\). If the asset provides income at the rate \\(q\\), its price must grow at the rate \\(r-q\\), so that the expected price after one step is \\(S_0e^{(r-q)\\Delta t}\\). The parameters \\(u\\) and \\(d\\) are unchanged, and Result 10.2 holds with a different value of \\(a\\).\nTable 10.1 The growth factor \\(a\\) in \\(p=(a-d)/(u-d)\\)\nUnderlying asset\\(a\\)Reason\nShare paying no dividend\\(e^{r\\Delta t}\\)The price grows at the risk-free rate\nStock index with dividend yield \\(q\\)\\(e^{(r-q)\\Delta t}\\)Part of the return is received as dividends\nCurrency with foreign rate \\(r_f\\)\\(e^{(r-r_f)\\Delta t}\\)The holder of the currency earns the foreign rate\nFutures contract\\(1\\)A futures position requires no investment, so its expected gain is zero"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.7#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.7", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.7", "heading": "Options on other assets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.7 Options on other assets", "body": "In every case the option is discounted at the risk-free rate \\(r\\). For an index with a volatility of 20 percent and a dividend yield of 2 percent, with \\(r=5\\) percent and steps of three months, \\(u=e^{0.20\\times0.5}=1.1052\\), \\(d=0.9048\\), \\(a=e^{0.03\\times0.25}=1.007528\\), and \\(p=(1.007528-0.9048)/(1.1052-0.9048)=0.5126\\).\nConcept check 10.3\nTwo analysts value the same option on a binomial tree with the same volatility. One believes that the share will return 6 percent per year, the other 16 percent. How do their valuations of the option compare?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.7#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.7", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.7", "heading": "Options on other assets", "kind": "notes", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.7 Options on other assets", "body": "Historical note\nThe binomial model was published in 1979 by John Cox, Stephen Ross, and Mark Rubinstein, six years after the Black–Scholes–Merton formula, and independently in the same year by Richard Rendleman and Brit Bartter. The authors credit the idea to William Sharpe, who is said to have proposed it as a way of explaining option valuation without the mathematics of continuous time. The model proved to be more than an expository device: because it values American options, for which no formula exists, it became and remains one of the principal numerical methods in practice.\nThe principle of risk-neutral valuation was stated by John Cox and Stephen Ross in 1976. Its mathematical basis, the existence of a set of probabilities under which the discounted price of every traded asset is its expected future value, was established by Michael Harrison and David Kreps in 1979, and it is in that form that the principle appears in advanced texts."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.summary#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.summary", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.summary Summary", "body": "On a one-step tree, a portfolio of \\(\\Delta=(f_u-f_d)/(S_0u-S_0d)\\) shares and risk-free borrowing replicates any option, and the value of the option is the cost of that portfolio.\nEquivalently, \\(f=e^{-rT}[pf_u+(1-p)f_d]\\) with \\(p=(e^{rT}-d)/(u-d)\\): the expected payoff in a risk-neutral world, discounted at the risk-free rate. The probability of a rise in the real world and the expected return on the share do not enter.\nTrees with several steps are valued by backward induction. For an American option, the value at each node is the greater of the exercise value and the continuation value.\nDelta changes from node to node, so that a replicating portfolio must be adjusted as the share price moves; the adjustment is self-financing.\nA tree matches a volatility of \\(\\sigma\\) when \\(u=e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}\\) and \\(d=1/u\\). As the number of steps increases, the value of a European option converges to the Black–Scholes–Merton value.\nFor an asset with income at rate \\(q\\), \\(a=e^{(r-q)\\Delta t}\\) replaces \\(e^{r\\Delta t}\\) in the probability; for a futures contract, \\(a=1\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.exercises Exercises", "body": "10.1 A share trades at $50. In six months it will be worth $55 or $45. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. Value a six-month European call with a strike of $50 by constructing the replicating portfolio, and verify the result with Result 10.1.\n\\(\\Delta=(5-0)/(55-45)=0.5\\). The portfolio of 0.5 shares must be worth 0 when the share is $45, so the loan repayment is \\(0.5\\times45=22.50\\) and the amount borrowed \\(22.50e^{-0.02}=\\$22.05\\). Cost: \\(0.5\\times50-22.05=\\$2.95\\). Check: \\(p=(e^{0.02}-0.9)/0.2=0.6010\\); \\(f=e^{-0.02}\\times0.6010\\times5=\\$2.95\\).\n10.2 On the tree of Exercise 10.1, value a six-month European put with a strike of $52 and verify put–call parity against the call with the same strike.\nPut: \\(f_u=0\\), \\(f_d=7\\); \\(f=e^{-0.02}\\times0.3990\\times7=\\$2.74\\). Call with strike $52: \\(f_u=3\\), \\(f_d=0\\); \\(f=e^{-0.02}\\times0.6010\\times3=\\$1.77\\). Parity: \\(c+Ke^{-rT}-S_0=1.77+52e^{-0.02}-50=1.77+50.97-50=\\$2.74\\).\n10.3 A share trades at $40. In each of two six-month steps it rises by 10 percent or falls by 10 percent. The risk-free rate is 6 percent. Value a one-year put with a strike of $42 (a) if European and (b) if American."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.exercises Exercises", "body": "\\(p=(e^{0.03}-0.9)/0.2=0.6523\\); discount factor \\(e^{-0.03}=0.9704\\). Final prices: 48.40, 39.60, 32.40; payoffs 0, 2.40, 9.60. Upper node ($44): \\(0.9704\\times0.3477\\times2.40=0.810\\); exercise value negative. Lower node ($36): continuation \\(0.9704\\times(0.6523\\times2.40+0.3477\\times9.60)=4.759\\); exercise value 6.00. (a) European: \\(0.9704\\times(0.6523\\times0.810+0.3477\\times4.759)=\\$2.12\\). (b) American: \\(0.9704\\times(0.6523\\times0.810+0.3477\\times6.00)=\\$2.54\\), which exceeds the immediate exercise value of $2.00.\n10.4 In Worked example 10.1, describe the replicating portfolio at the initial node and show that, if the share price rises in the first step, the portfolio is worth exactly the amount required to establish the replicating portfolio for the second step."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.exercises Exercises", "body": "At the initial node \\(\\Delta=0.587\\) and the call is worth $2.259, so the portfolio holds 0.587 shares (\\(0.5874\\times28=\\$16.448\\)) financed by borrowing \\(16.448-2.259=\\$14.189\\). After a rise, the shares are worth \\(0.5874\\times32.20=\\$18.915\\) and the debt is \\(14.189e^{0.0125}=\\$14.367\\); the portfolio is worth \\(18.915-14.367=\\$4.548\\), which is \\(f_u\\). At that node \\(\\Delta=1.000\\): the portfolio must hold one share ($32.20) with borrowing of \\(32.20-4.548=\\$27.652\\). The additional 0.4126 shares cost \\(0.4126\\times32.20=\\$13.285\\), which is exactly the increase in borrowing, \\(27.652-14.367\\). No new money is required.\n10.5 A share has a volatility of 30 percent. The risk-free rate is 5 percent. Find \\(u\\), \\(d\\), and \\(p\\) for a tree with steps of one month.\n\\(\\Delta t=1/12\\). \\(u=e^{0.30\\sqrt{1/12}}=e^{0.0866}=1.0905\\); \\(d=0.9170\\); \\(a=e^{0.05/12}=1.004175\\); \\(p=(1.004175-0.9170)/(1.0905-0.9170)=0.5024\\).\n10.6 In the opening exercise of the chapter, the call is quoted at $2.40. Set out the arbitrage and show the result in both outcomes."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises#34", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 34, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.exercises Exercises", "body": "The call is dear. Write the call for $2.40, buy 0.60 shares for $16.80, and borrow $14.93: net receipt today \\(2.40-16.80+14.93=\\$0.53\\). If the share rises: shares worth \\(0.60\\times32.20=19.32\\); call costs 4.20; loan requires 15.12; net \\(19.32-4.20-15.12=0\\). If it falls: shares worth 15.12; call expires; loan requires 15.12; net 0. The arbitrageur keeps $0.53 with no obligation.\n10.7 A futures price is 80 and its volatility 25 percent. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. Using a one-step tree of six months with the parameters of Result 10.2 and Table 10.1, value a six-month European call on the futures with a strike of 80.\n\\(u=e^{0.25\\sqrt{0.5}}=e^{0.17678}=1.1934\\); \\(d=0.8380\\); \\(a=1\\); \\(p=(1-0.8380)/(1.1934-0.8380)=0.4559\\). \\(F_u=95.47\\), payoff 15.47; \\(F_d=67.04\\), payoff 0. \\(f=e^{-0.02}\\times0.4559\\times15.47=\\$6.91\\).\n10.8 “Risk-neutral valuation assumes that investors do not care about risk, which is false; the prices it gives are therefore unreliable.” Evaluate this statement."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises#35", "source_key": "fd-textbook:10.exercises", "chapter": 10, "section": "10.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 35, "title": "Chapter 10, The Binomial Model — 10.exercises Exercises", "body": "The statement misdescribes the principle. The value of an option is established by replication: the option is worth the cost of a portfolio of the share and risk-free borrowing that reproduces it, and that cost is the same whatever investors' attitudes to risk, since those attitudes are already reflected in the price of the share. Because the value does not depend on attitudes to risk, it may be computed under any assumed attitude, and the assumption of risk neutrality is chosen for convenience: expected returns all equal the risk-free rate and discounting is at that rate. The result is the correct price in the real world. The principle would fail only where replication fails, for example if the share could not be traded."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
